ELINES ......................................................... 9-100\nL284@P6: Exhibit 9.3-R RIGHT-TURN POCKET OR TAPER ....................................................... 9-101\nL285@P6: Exhibit 9.3-S RECOMMENDED MINIMUM LENGTHS FOR BYPASS LANES............. 9-103\nL286@P6: Exhibit 9.5-A DESIRABLE AND MAXIMUM SLOPES .................................................. 9-134\nL287@P6: Exhibit 9.5-B ENHANCED CUT SLOPE END TREATMENT........................................ 9-141\nL288@P6-8: Exhibit 9.5-C SERRATED SLOPES ............................................................................. 9-142This page intentionally left blank.Highway Design August 2012\nL289@P8: General 9-1\nL290@P8: CHAPTER 9\nL291@P8: HIGHWAY DESIGN\nL292@P8: 9.1 GENERAL\nL293@P8: This chapter provides policies, standards, standard practices, criteria, guidance and references\nL294@P8: for developing and documenting the highway design. This includes development of the final\nL295@P8: geometric design and the preparation of plans, specifications and estimates (PS&E) and related\nL296@P8: information to support highway construction and subsequent facility operations. The highway\nL297@P8: design policies and standards are applicable to new highway construction and reconstruction,\nL298@P8: as well as Resurfacing, Restoration and Rehabilitation (RRR) improvements. Section 1.1.1\nL299@P8: provides policy definitions, standards, standard practices, criteria, and guidance. FLH Policy\nL300@P8: statements are shown in bold type. Statements regarding FLH Standard Practice are so\nL301@P8: indicated. Information on how to perform basic design procedures and fundamental steps for\nL302@P8: performing the design work are typically incorporated by references to other documents.\nL303@P8: Refer to [EFLHD – CFLHD – WFLHD] Division Supplements for more information.\nL304@P8: 9.1.1 ROLE OF THE DESIGNER\nL305@P8: The role and responsibility of the highway designer is to gather and incorporate all of the\nL306@P8: interdisciplinary engineering and environmental input required to develop the highway design,\nL307@P8: and to provide a complete and acceptable PS&E assembly with all appropriate supporting\nL308@P8: documentation. The highway design and the PS&E package represents the final product of a\nL309@P8: collaborative, interdisciplinary and interagency design effort and depicts all the various decisions\nL310@P8: and commitments made during the planning, programming and project development processes.\nL311@P8: The designer is responsible for participating in an interdisciplinary (e.g., cross-functional) team\nL312@P8: approach, led by the Project Manager, for evaluating design issues and developing design\nL313@P8: solutions for the project delivery. The designer is responsible for interpreting and applying\nL314@P8: guidance from each chapter in the PDDM as applicable to highway design development and\nL315@P8: PS&E compilation. The designer is also responsible for directly incorporating certain\nL316@P8: engineering data, plans, specifications and estimates produced by the other engineering\nL317@P8: disciplines into the PS&E and assuring that these products properly interrelate within the final\nL318@P8: PS&E assembly. The following briefly summarizes the designer’s role and responsibility for\nL319@P8: development of the geometric design and PS&E, in relation to other disciplines described in the\nL320@P8: respective chapters of the PDDM.\nL321@P8: ● Chapter 1 – Introduction. Incorporate the philosophy, technical policies, and general\nL322@P8: approach to risk while developing the highway design and preparation of the PS&E.\nL323@P8: ● Chapter 2 – Planning and Programming. Designers should use information\nL324@P8: developed during the planning and programming phases including interagency\nL325@P8: agreements, planning and inventory data, program information and other general data\nL326@P8-9: developed on the scope, schedule and funding amount for the project being designed.Highway Design August 2012\nL327@P9: 9-2 General\nL328@P9: ● Chapter 3 – Environmental Stewardship. Incorporate information regarding\nL329@P9: environmental requirements and public involvement. Environmental documents include\nL330@P9: the decisions and commitments made for mitigation of project impacts and concerns.\nL331@P9: Review all environmental documents for decisions, mitigation measures and\nL332@P9: commitments made during the conceptual studies and preliminary design phase that\nL333@P9: affect development and construction of the project or operation of the highway following\nL334@P9: construction. Coordinate any proposed deviation from the decisions, mitigation\nL335@P9: measures and commitments with the Environmental Section and affected resource\nL336@P9: agencies.\n"
